19
00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.970
And I think first, we should kind of synopsize it

20
00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.040
for people that have not seen the movie.

21
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.850 line:15%
So, I guess I can allow you guys--

22
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:02.683 line:15%
Sure.

23
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:04.087 line:15%
The opportunity to tell us a little bit

24
00:01:04.087 --> 00:01:05.010 line:15%
about the delay.

25
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.930 line:15%
You'd think after like months of explaining this

26
00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:07.870
we'd be really good at it,

27
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.080
but it's always very nerve wrecking,

28
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.550
it'd be like, "What is this movie actually about?"

29
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.921
It really depends on the day.

30
00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:12.786
It's about so many things.

31
00:01:12.786 --> 00:01:13.830
Exactly.

32
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.910
It's hard to like nail down specifically what it's about.

33
00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.400
I mean, there's things that it's about on the surface

34
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.750
but what the movie is really about is what's going on

35
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.220
beneath that veneer of what's happening specifically

36
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.200
with "Pepe The Frog".

37
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.400
Exactly
Yeah.

38
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.610
And I mean, I think it's that complexity that drew us

39
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.070
to the story initially,

40
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.080
but you know, the inciting incident for the movie

41
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.420
is a pretty simple and kind of weird thing.

42
00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.840
It's a single panel of an obscure comic book

43
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.300
called "Boys Club".

44
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.850
And there's this one panel in which Pepe, the frog

45
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.890
which is this anthropomorphic stone green frog character.

46
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:49.830
That if you haven't seen "Pepe The Frog"

47
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.582
you just stop the podcast and then Google it

48
00:01:53.582 --> 00:01:55.570
and then immediately come back, close that tab.

49
00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.248
Come back in two weeks.

50
00:01:57.248 --> 00:02:00.060
You finally wrap your head around what exactly is going on.

51
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.300
After you've taken a disinfection shower

52
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.900
and then come back to the podcast.

53
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.750
But so there's this one panel

54
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.670
of this comic that's done by Matt Furie.

55
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.290
Who's the subject of our documentary.

56
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.850
And in this panel, Pepe is saying, "Feels good, man."

57
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.960
And it's this one single image

58
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.550
of Pepe that was cut and paste out of that image

59
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.950
and then became this like viral phenomenon globally.

60
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.870
And our story tells the very like strange

61
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.240
and unlikely journey of this comic book

62
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.870
character becoming an internet meme that was wildly popular

63
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.550
and then ultimately becoming sort of a tool

64
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.830
and a propaganda sort of tool

65
00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.550
for the alt-right and officially designated a hate symbol

66
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.490
by the Anti-Defamation league in late 2016.

67
00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.410
And the story is kind of two-fold, it's the story

68
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.170
of Matt Furie who created this with no ill intent

69
00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.830
obviously it becoming a meme

70
00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.600
and then a hate symbol had nothing to do with him.

71
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.290
And it's the story of him sort of realizing that

72
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.220
and then dealing with it

73
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.920
in the ways that he felt were correct to him.

74
00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.070
And then the other part

75
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.930
of the story is about how trolling moved

76
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.020
off of message boards and into mainstream politics

77
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.898
because Pepe was really used as basically a tool for trolls.

78
00:03:07.898 --> 00:03:10.380
And so when we started making the film,

79
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.250
we realized that this could be like a really unique story

80
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.900
because the way that Pepe was sort of like taken

81
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.210
from Matt was a way that we felt like a lot

82
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.750
of viewers would potentially see consensus reality

83
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.080
sort of being stolen from them

84
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.760
in the attention economy.
Right.

85
00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:29.760
And so it was something that also myself as a cartoonist

86
00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.560
was excited to just have a movie that talked

87
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.290
about cartoon seriously.
Right.

88
00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.680
You know, in the way that like cartoons carry all

89
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.140
of this kind of like visual baggage to them,

90
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.620
that they are these things that are like intellectually

91
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.100
sticky, that people often sort of dismiss, but, you know

92
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.460
they're important and potent and stay with us for decades.

93
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.670
And Pepe was this, I don't know emotionally flexible avatar

94
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.640
that people were using.

95
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.340
And speaking of cartoons seriously,

96
00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.020
and as important as they are the panel

97
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.230
in which Pepe says "Feels Good Man"

98
00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.130
is in response to his friends making fun of him

99
00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.220
for peeing with his pants around his ankle right?

100
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.053
So--

101
00:04:08.053 --> 00:04:12.500
It's perhaps like the most benign like innocent, you know,

102
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.430
ridiculous thing ever.

103
00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.430
And the idea that that image could get co-opted

104
00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.060
and evolve over time into such a, you know

105
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.060
pernicious icon of regressive culture is baffling, right.

106
00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.940
And I just remember personally the first time

107
00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.090
I came across "Pepe The Frog" late in the game

108
00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.340
was just seeing that emoji popping up

109
00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.260
on people's Twitter profiles

110
00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.970
and being used in posts that were, you know

111
00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.360
very much in the alt-right bent and being confused,

112
00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:47.200
like why is this frog being aligned

113
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.350
with this particular political perspective

114
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.910
doing a little bit of research into it

115
00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.700
but still not really grokking what exactly was going on.

116
00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.230
And the story that you tell is just so much more astounding

117
00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:02.660
and exasperating than you could possibly imagine.

118
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.120
Yeah and I think that's sort of how I understood

119
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.300
Pepe at the beginning.

120
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.700
Like I had spent a lot of time on Reddit in the early days

121
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.500
and like was familiar certainly with Pepe as a meme

122
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.780
but when Arthur told me that he was starting this project

123
00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.380
like it came from a relationship

124
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.260
that he personally had with Matt.

125
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.130
And so like, I felt kind of totally embarrassed

126
00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.840
that I didn't know anything about the backstory,

127
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.960
but like in those first early conversations

128
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:29.860
it was just very clear

129
00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.310
that this was such an incredible opportunity to tell

130
00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:35.110
at least what I find like, to be the most interesting kind

131
00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.920
of documentaries that are about something on its face

132
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.530
very specific and eccentric and strange

133
00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:41.370
but really speak to like

134
00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:44.980
a broader cultural situation.
Right.

135
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:46.770
Yeah, in the broadest sense,

136
00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.907
it's about how information travels

137
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.820
and shapes culture, right

138
00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:59.820
through the lens of this one specific image, but you know

139
00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.680
the idea of the meme, you know,

140
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.000
as it's most broadly defined

141
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.650
and I love this woman that you have on who, you know

142
00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.280 line:15%
the color with all the crazy hair

143
00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.080 line:15%
and everything like that, 'cause she's a character, right.

144
00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.280 line:15%
And she's into like the paranormal and all this crazy stuff.

145
00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.640
But the idea--
Susan Blackmore.

146
00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.700
The idea of the meme hearkening back to Richard Dawkins

147
00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.110
and this book that he wrote

148
00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.660
that there are genes that influence human behavior.

149
00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.090
And its memes that influence cultural behavior

150
00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:26.990
and evolve over time to, you know

151
00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.800
shape not just political philosophy and ideology

152
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.020
but cultural identity

153
00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.970
and everything that gets packed into that.

154
00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.630
Like the power of that cannot be overstated.

155
00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.900
And yet we kind of think of memes as a very, you know

156
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.130
kind of casual, temporal thing.

157
00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.523
Oh yeah, we're just beginning to understand

158
00:06:45.523 --> 00:06:48.920
like the true grotesque potential

159
00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.840
to like coalesce political bodies

160
00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.970
but also be deployed as political propaganda.

161
00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.950
There's just so much embedded within the way images

162
00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:02.980
operate on the internet.

163
00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.440
Yeah, this has definitely had its core.

164
00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.400
The film is like a really immediate study

165
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.800
or media literacy project.
Right.

166
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.240
It kind of in the back of my head

167
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.220
when we were starting at two, I was observing how my father

168
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.970
like I grew up in rural Missouri

169
00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.720
and very conservative family--
Evangelical, right?

170
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.540
Yeah, evangelical family, Southern Baptists.

171
00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.800
And my dad had been like a Bob Dole supporter.

172
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.800
He'd been like kind of a family values conservative.

173
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.940
And he's a very like moral guy.

174
00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.430
Like he's never had a beer or a cigarette, you know

175
00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.040
he's someone that really walks the walk.

176
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.980
And I would thought that he would've been shocked

177
00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.260
and horrified by Trump.

178
00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.310
I would have thought that he would have found Trump's morals

179
00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.650
to be reprehensible.

180
00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:50.180
And I was surprised that all of a sudden

181
00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:52.727
he was really kind of completely enamored with Trump.

182
00:07:52.727 --> 00:07:55.500
And I was also surprised how he just got an iPhone

183
00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.480
and when it stopped staring at it

184
00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.580
he was using the iPhone, like a teenage girl.

185
00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.190
Like he was just scrolling constantly

186
00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.740
the Fox news media feed.

187
00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:07.420
And it was something that I was kind of trying to like think

188
00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.253
about and deal with.

189
00:08:08.253 --> 00:08:09.750
And even though none of that is like in the film

190
00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:13.760
specifically that was the stuff that I was thinking

191
00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.180
about as we were beginning to make the film

192
00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.770
how can we have this story that's about these?

193
00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.670
For me, it was kind of like this twin passion

194
00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.960
of like cartoons and iconography

195
00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.890
and then also kind of trying to decode what is going on

196
00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.650
in the country.
Right.

197
00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.903
And so, you know those were the conversations

198
00:08:28.903 --> 00:08:31.450
that Giorgio and I were having about in the beginning

199
00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:35.730
and we thought Pepe was just such an amazing vehicle

200
00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.060
for this larger discussion.

201
00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:38.690
'Cause ultimately we can make a movie

202
00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.070
about this hilarious stoned, weird frog.

203
00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.880
And then we can sort of like within all of that,

204
00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:47.230
have all of this like really kind of important

205
00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.510
informational stuff that is pointing

206
00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.340
towards a seismic shift in culture.

207
00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:53.260
Social media is changing culture in a way

208
00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.627
that we are just beginning to understand, you know,

209
00:08:55.627 --> 00:08:58.050
it "Feels Good Man" is like a cave painting at the beginning

210
00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.350
of a new era.
Right.

211
00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.070
You know, and I think it's just like the way the internet

212
00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:06.050
is kind of like downloading our psyches into the machine

213
00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.500
is something we have to be aware of.

214
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.260
And Pepe is like a way

215
00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.370
in which we can kind of like

216
00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:11.203
talk about it.
Right.

217
00:09:11.203 --> 00:09:14.250
It doesn't feel like overly intellectualized

218
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.047
it doesn't feel like, you know, too important.

219
00:09:18.047 --> 00:09:18.880
It's still this kind

220
00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.230
of like weird frog.
Right.

221
00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.380
And yeah, it was just something

222
00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:25.807
that we just became completely obsessed with over the two

223
00:09:25.807 --> 00:09:27.440
and a half years we were making it.

224
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.370
It also underscores just how strange the whole thing

225
00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.601
is because Pepe is this bizarre, you know, figure,

226
00:09:35.601 --> 00:09:39.530
it's like it's this amazing entry point to explore

227
00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:42.030
a very serious subject with this veneer

228
00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:43.320
of kind of lightness

229
00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.890
at the same time, right.

230
00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:49.850
So you Arthur, your background is as a cartoonist.

231
00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:51.990
Yeah, like--
Yeah and graphic designer.

232
00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.500
So you were friends with Matt, like take us back

233
00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.680
to I wanna talk about Matt a little bit,

234
00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.850
like and the impetus for the film, you know, at the outset.

235
00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:00.800
Well, to go back to the beginning

236
00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.470
is I'd bought his comic book boys club at Quimby's Bookstore

237
00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.000
in Chicago, and that's an independent bookstore

238
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.510
and it was a pretty obscure comic.

239
00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.010
It's not a comic book store that you would buy

240
00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.670
like a Spiderman, you know,

241
00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:13.900
an issue of Spiderman.
Right.

242
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.390
This was more of like a, you know

243
00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.970
bespoke kind of indie store.

244
00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.060
And I thought his comic was really funny

245
00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.200
and I really liked it.

246
00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.850
And it had stayed with me for several years.

247
00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.190
Just kind of like in the back of my head.

248
00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.450
So when I saw Pepe start to pop up on the internet,

249
00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:29.630
I found it baffling.

250
00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:32.790
I wondered if the people who are using it had any knowledge

251
00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.583
of its context or the comic.

252
00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.060
And then I met Matt when I moved to California

253
00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.400
through some mutual friends.

254
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.250
He was friends with my girlfriend, Carrie McLaughlin.

255
00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:44.360
Who's a co-producer on the film.

256
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:46.260
And actually some of the archival footage used

257
00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.740
in "Feels Good Man" was stuff that she had shot.

258
00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:52.100
And we took a hike to a hot Springs.

259
00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:52.933
It took two days.

260
00:10:52.933 --> 00:10:56.010
We hiked out like 10 or 12 miles with a group of friends.

261
00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.090
We stayed at a hot Springs overnight.

262
00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.390
And then we like hiked back.

263
00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.410
And it was something that Matt and I just bonded.

264
00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:05.150
We bonded over cartoons.

265
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:08.660
We bonded over the things that we were most excited about.

266
00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.820
And then we started to run

267
00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:13.740
into each other around Los Angeles.

268
00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.640
And when I'd run into him, I would try to figure

269
00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.270
out ways to talk about Pepe a little bit.

270
00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:18.587
'Cause I'd see him in the news and be like

271
00:11:18.587 --> 00:11:19.904
"What do you think about this?"

272
00:11:19.904 --> 00:11:23.050
And I could tell that Matt's opinion

273
00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.550
about it was still forming.

274
00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.010
It was something where initially I think

275
00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:27.580
he just wanted to like ignore it.

276
00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:29.590
And he just figured this was just kind of like part

277
00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.840
of the internet that he had nothing to do with.

278
00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.140
And then as it started to get weirder and weirder

279
00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:39.160
he just kind of, as a friend just asked me for help

280
00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:42.120
he could see that I was like, maybe thinking

281
00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:46.250
about this a lot and that he trusted me.

282
00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.130
And so initially we thought about trying to produce

283
00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:52.190
like a cartoon and the cartoon would be sort of an updating

284
00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.023
of boys club.

285
00:11:53.023 --> 00:11:54.320
It be, we would take that as sort

286
00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.110
of the initial source material

287
00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:59.100
and then we would tell sort of like a Alison Wonderland

288
00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:02.030
kind of story, where Pepe gets sucked out of the comic book

289
00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.510
and then into this like kind of dystopian existence.

290
00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:06.560
And he asked to find his way back home.

291
00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.010
And then we pitched that around Los Angeles

292
00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:13.270
and surprisingly no one saw that--

293
00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:14.583
But I watched a little,

294
00:12:14.583 --> 00:12:16.573
the part in the Pepe evolution take place.

295
00:12:16.573 --> 00:12:18.050
This is like 2015.

296
00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.840
So like, yeah.

297
00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:20.673
So it's insane

298
00:12:20.673 --> 00:12:21.680
at this point.
Yeah.

299
00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:22.909
It was 2016.

300
00:12:22.909 --> 00:12:24.000
It was a little bit insane for us

301
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.730
to do it for sure.
Right.

302
00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:28.850
Just because you have to realize some people

303
00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:30.700
thought Matt was somehow like a member

304
00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:31.860
of the alt-right.
Right.

305
00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.610
They would see his comics.
Of course.

306
00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.030
They would just all of a sudden be like,

307
00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:36.073
oh no, no, no, we can't go there.

308
00:12:36.073 --> 00:12:39.661
And that's the thing that's baffling about the ADL stuff.

309
00:12:39.661 --> 00:12:44.040
When Pepe gets listed as a symbol of hate speech

310
00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.210
they put his name alongside it, which was confusing

311
00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.470
because then it really did align him with that.

312
00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.010
And that had to be unbelievably painful for him

313
00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.360
but couldn't they just get rid of the reference

314
00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:56.958
to him personally?

315
00:12:56.958 --> 00:12:58.920
They've rewritten--
They have, they have.

316
00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.863
The entry a number of times.

317
00:13:00.863 --> 00:13:02.780
And you know, the ADL has, you know

318
00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:04.590
we've talked to them at various points during the making

319
00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.730
of the film and they've been pretty generous with us.

320
00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:10.470
I think they were just kind of trying to do

321
00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:11.881
what they felt was best in the moment

322
00:13:11.881 --> 00:13:13.610
and did not think about the repercussions.

323
00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.100
I think the issues Matt had with us,

324
00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:16.610
they just didn't reach out to tell him

325
00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:18.040
that this was about to happen.

326
00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.430
And all of a sudden Matt wakes up one day

327
00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:21.840
and he's a news item.

328
00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:25.440
And he is obviously like horrified

329
00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.010
that his name was attached to this.

330
00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:29.955
And also it's just like, he's trying to eat his career

331
00:13:29.955 --> 00:13:32.590
out basically as an independent cartoonist.

332
00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:33.423
And all of a sudden

333
00:13:33.423 --> 00:13:36.580
if you're the guy that popularized the alt-right if that's

334
00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.940
what people perceive--
you are radioactive.

335
00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:40.640
Completely, completely.

336
00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:43.530
And so, you know, that was another motivation

337
00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:44.890
for us wanting to make the film was

338
00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:49.170
'cause we just realized that almost no one knew Matt story.

339
00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:51.520
They did not understand where Pepe came from.

340
00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:53.430
And we thought that like if people understood

341
00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:56.190
the true context for the character and the backstory

342
00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.710
that we would basically be able to, like you know,

343
00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:01.000
we've talked about it before

344
00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.390
as like kind of like canonized Pepe.

345
00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.210
So at least people would know

346
00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:06.620
that if they see a meme of Pepe

347
00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.600
that's not the original version

348
00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.110
'cause there's been a lot of cartoons

349
00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:13.833
that have been remixed, redrawn in really hateful ways.

350
00:14:13.833 --> 00:14:14.790
Like there's a lot

351
00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:16.310
of like racist SpongeBob--
Right.

352
00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:17.610
On the internet.

353
00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.080
But the difference is that yeah

354
00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:22.060
the difference is that there's a huge studio that created it

355
00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.270
and everybody has a prior context for that.

356
00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.510
But boys club was this hidden little thing

357
00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:28.080
that almost nobody knew about.

358
00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:30.120
So they didn't have that history

359
00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:31.750
to contextualize the whole thing.

360
00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.120
No I mean, it's kind of remarkable.

361
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:37.920
If you look back on it as, you know, the full totality

362
00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.557
of the story, like Matt creates Pepe,

363
00:14:40.557 --> 00:14:41.553
what in 2006?
Yeah.

364
00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.970
And I don't have the numbers, but I would guess

365
00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.040
that Pepe is probably like in the top 10

366
00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:50.750
most recognizable cartoon images across the world.

367
00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:55.750
And it has nothing to do with like a company, just, you know

368
00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.950
pumping billions of dollars into it as a brand.

369
00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:02.220
It's just purely in the power of Matt's initial image

370
00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.830
and its ability to have like connected

371
00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.750
with a particular audience online

372
00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.970
and just grown from there and, you know, dimension like it

373
00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:10.440
started off innocently.
Right.

374
00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.940
It really metastasized into something much different

375
00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:16.240
but like in a sense

376
00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.870
the film was trying to reverse engineer that context

377
00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.700
that on the internet, in the absence of that context,

378
00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:25.810
like the hive, mine of the internet

379
00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:28.040
just kind of injected its own narrative into Pepe.

380
00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.860
And so I think we're happy to see

381
00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.040
that in the release of the film, it's kind of operated

382
00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.420
just as we would have hoped that like it's really

383
00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:39.737
that's all Matt's ever really wanted to do

384
00:15:39.737 --> 00:15:42.280
was to tell a story, not to like, I think sometimes

385
00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:44.500
he gets castigated by certain trolls online

386
00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:47.470
for like being naive about wanting to like take back Pepe.

387
00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:49.610
I don't think there's not an interest in taking it back.

388
00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.260
It's just telling its story.

389
00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:53.810
Right, yeah, I mean, there is a narrative

390
00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:58.810
that he was somewhat naive and thinking initially that,

391
00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.720
you know, it would just go away

392
00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.920
until it gets so out of control that he's, you know

393
00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:07.420
realize that he's got to step in and do something.

394
00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:11.000
So the underlying theme is really this journey

395
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.890
from passivity to participation on behalf

396
00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:17.950
of Matt specifically, but also on behalf of, you know

397
00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:20.620
the 4chan community and all these other people

398
00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:25.240
who become activated to participate in the political process

399
00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:29.950
in a way that previously, you know, didn't appeal to them

400
00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:32.700
because of feeling disenfranchised or whatever.

401
00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.880
So that's the real power that kind of engine

402
00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.870
beneath the whole thing that created this metastasized

403
00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.230
you know, icon and also,

404
00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:44.720
the result of the 2016 election in so many ways.

405
00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:47.640
Yeah, I mean Matt sort of alone in that experience,

406
00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:49.210
right there aren't really many other artists

407
00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.140
that have had to go through what he's gone through.

408
00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.800
And so he's really had to make up the game plan

409
00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:55.633
along the way.

410
00:16:55.633 --> 00:16:59.420
And so as documentary filmmakers, it was really

411
00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:01.840
a gift to be able to capture that sort of coming

412
00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:03.850
off the proverbial couch moment happening

413
00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:04.683
in real time, right.

414
00:17:04.683 --> 00:17:08.570
To really track someone, have to confront something

415
00:17:08.570 --> 00:17:10.870
that they thought would just go away on its own

416
00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:14.310
and really feel like they're kind of this reluctant hero.

417
00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:17.720
And that as filmmakers was really potentially powerful

418
00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:19.120
because I think for a lot of Americans

419
00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:21.010
they might see themselves in him a bit, right.

420
00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.290
Like in the way that his artwork

421
00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:25.290
was taken away from them there.

422
00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.250
Yeah, like Arthur just mentioned like their sense

423
00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:31.030
of making sense of the world had been taken away from them.

424
00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:35.010
Yeah I mean, you see this guy, you know, this expectation

425
00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.780
that he's gonna hire a lawyer, the incident he finds out

426
00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:40.980
that Pepe is, you know, being used in a way

427
00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:43.200
that he didn't intend is preposterous.

428
00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.490
It's like, this is, you know, he's a very sensitive

429
00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:48.610
artists seems like a really sweet guy.

430
00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:50.993
Like that's not the kind of guy who's gonna do that, right.

431
00:17:50.993 --> 00:17:53.244
So that stuff costs money, you know?

432
00:17:53.244 --> 00:17:56.030
Yeah, exactly, you know--
Costs a lot of money.

433
00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.610
In a weird way, it had to get as bad as it got--

434
00:17:58.610 --> 00:17:59.590
And nobody could have predicted

435
00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.561
that anyway.
Exactly, you know, so.

436
00:18:01.561 --> 00:18:05.370
I understand why people might consider Matt naive

437
00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:07.790
but I think it's important to point out that like

438
00:18:07.790 --> 00:18:09.700
obviously this has never happened before

439
00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.593
and Pepe has outlived a lot of the platforms

440
00:18:11.593 --> 00:18:14.580
that was even popular on things on the internet do disappear

441
00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:16.900
and Pepe for whatever reason has stuck around now

442
00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:18.040
for more than a decade.
Right.

443
00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:19.960
And so I think Matt initially thinking

444
00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:21.740
that it would go away the same way MySpace

445
00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.410
and Friendster did made sense.

446
00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.980
And then also it's kind of important to point out

447
00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.120
we documented the film is that Matt was a new father

448
00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:31.290
when all of this was happening.

449
00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.070
He was a lot more concerned with providing for his family

450
00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.510
and changing diapers than necessarily

451
00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.270
taking on the alt-right.
Right.

452
00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:42.110
And so he just kind of didn't have like the emotional

453
00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:44.710
or financial bandwidth to deal with it.

454
00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:49.420
And so, you know, I think he's in the film

455
00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:53.180
he realized at a certain point that this obviously

456
00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:55.130
he had to use copyright

457
00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.140
because it was kind of the only tool that he had.

458
00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:59.380
But before that, he tried initially to reach

459
00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.800
out to his artistic community, to remix

460
00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:05.480
and re kind of meme Pepe in a positive way.

461
00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:06.800
And I think that's the moment

462
00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.910
that people often would say Matt was naive

463
00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.220
but I think Matt knew that going into it.

464
00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.170
And it was a way for him basically just to reach out

465
00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.170
to his community in that moment and say like,

466
00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:16.770
hey, I need help.
Yeah.

467
00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.120
You know, can you help me?

468
00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:20.290
And it was a way for him to talk to his friends

469
00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:22.890
about it and then for them to make artwork about it.

470
00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.600
And it was like a very earnest thing.

471
00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.890
Yeah and ultimately something like 500, you know

472
00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:30.840
kind loving Pepes emerge from that.

473
00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:34.450
But you know, that's a drop in the sea of, you know

474
00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.060
hundreds of millions of, you know

475
00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:39.720
pernicious versions of that there's that scene

476
00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:41.510
near the end where he's in that, like

477
00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:43.590
it looks like kind of a think tank situation

478
00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.730
in San Francisco with all these data scientists

479
00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.450
and they're analyzing hate speech on the internet.

480
00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:51.850
And he says, there's like a hundred million store.

481
00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:55.080
It's by far the most, it's like, yeah, there's no way

482
00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.330
that you can, he's like I wish you luck with that.

483
00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.204
But you know, it was basically like, you know,

484
00:19:59.204 --> 00:20:01.420
this is not the way forward

485
00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:04.400
in terms of like shifting that perspective.

486
00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.790
But it's a shame that it, like, it had to get to a point

487
00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:08.790
that it reached such a fever pitch

488
00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:13.790
that this very large and powerful legal institution

489
00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.070
that WilmerHale Lewis, Tom Burrows and Stephanie Lynn

490
00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:19.640
were able to come help Matt pro bono.

491
00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:22.810
Right, like it had to get to a point at which a law firm

492
00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:24.760
could see a kind of greater social good

493
00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:26.470
and donating their time to help him.

494
00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.950
And without that, I mean, I don't know where he would be.

495
00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:32.080
And it was really like, I don't know if it's bears

496
00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:35.020
mentioning to you that there was this break point

497
00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:40.020
for Matt where, well, I guess now no longer

498
00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:44.010
assistant principal of a middle school in Texas

499
00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:45.950
had published this children's book

500
00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:48.850
that had once again co-opted Pepe,

501
00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:51.150
but in the context of like a pretty racist.

502
00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:54.660
Yeah, that's a bridge too far

503
00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:56.630
where he really got activated after that.

504
00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:59.790
Yeah I mean, it was propaganda directed at four

505
00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.750
and five-year-olds, and you know, in the movie

506
00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:04.880
we also showed that Matt is a children's book author

507
00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.810
an illustrator and I think he found the fact

508
00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:10.840
that this propaganda was sort of being pointed at

509
00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:11.960
such younger audiences.

510
00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:14.000
He found to be really disturbing.

511
00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.600
And it was also just easily accessible on Amazon.

512
00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:18.980
It wasn't just as simple as like asking Amazon

513
00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:20.605
to take down the book.

514
00:21:20.605 --> 00:21:22.970
And so, you know, we've even noticed that like, you know

515
00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:25.900
there's all this bootleg Pepe's stuff, that's on Amazon.

516
00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:28.220
It's actually quite hard to get them to take it down.

517
00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:31.500
You know, none of this is like just an easy process.

518
00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:34.370
It takes a lot of time and a lot of intentionality.

519
00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:37.203
And so he realized that that was kind of the moment

520
00:21:37.203 --> 00:21:39.530
that he needed just to seek out help.

521
00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.360
And yeah, luckily he found this law firm

522
00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:42.193
that was willing to do it,

523
00:21:42.193 --> 00:21:45.830
and they continue to kind of fight for Pepe

524
00:21:45.830 --> 00:21:47.740
in various kind of ways now.

525
00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:49.600
I love the arrow shot of the lawyer

526
00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:51.830
coming into the conference room.

527
00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:53.576
That got a standing ovation.

528
00:21:53.576 --> 00:21:55.403
Yeah.
Yeah.

529
00:21:56.323 --> 00:21:58.463
That was, yeah, that was an amazing moment of Sundance.

530
00:22:00.363 --> 00:22:03.680 line:15%
The Louis, the Bain lawyer there was sitting just down

531
00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:05.500 line:15%
the road at a Sundance.

532
00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.710
And when the hero shot happens, like what happened?

533
00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:11.260
I saw his wife like reach over and like squeezes.

534
00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.650
Yeah, there was, but yeah, it was a very validating moment

535
00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:18.901
for them to have like this audience, you know

536
00:22:18.901 --> 00:22:19.970
'cause people are ready in the movie

537
00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.380
for something to happen.
Right.

538
00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:22.410
'Cause we do sort of like--

539
00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:24.997
Well, cause it goes to such a dark and despairing place

540
00:22:24.997 --> 00:22:27.120
you know, and I was concerned about that.

541
00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:28.900
I was like, how are you gonna round this out?

542
00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:31.280
Like I wasn't aware of how it ended.

543
00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:33.670
And that was quite uplifting and interesting.

544
00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:36.210
And maybe we should leave that for audiences

545
00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:37.663
to discover themselves.

546
00:22:38.882 --> 00:22:40.840
But, but it is, it does end on a very hopeful note.

547
00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:43.970
But I think what I'd like to do is, is walk through

548
00:22:45.976 --> 00:22:48.473
a little bit of the timeline and the evolution of Pepe

549
00:22:48.473 --> 00:22:50.830
so that people can wrap their heads

550
00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:52.160
around what has happened.

551
00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:55.170
I mean, it basically begins, as you said, with him

552
00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:59.700
uploading a panel from his comic book to my space

553
00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:04.700
it then seems to get adopted by the kind of fitness

554
00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.650
subculture on the internet.

555
00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:10.010
Who start saying, feels good man after their workouts,

556
00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:12.070
which is an innocent and kind of fun thing.

557
00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:16.620
And were you able to actually connect the dots

558
00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:19.130
from that back to Matt?

559
00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.920
Well I mean, nothing on the internet is a straight line.

560
00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:23.210
It's sort of all spread out.

561
00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:27.380
I mean, you know, we weren't able to connect things back

562
00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:30.470
to like certain people or certain sort of moments on things

563
00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:34.100
but it did seem like there were a few message boards

564
00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:39.800
that one was about mushrooms and that kind of made sense.

565
00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:44.300
And then there was another one that was bodybuilding.com

566
00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:46.310
message board and for whatever reason

567
00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:48.730
that message board has always had a relationship

568
00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:51.410
to the fit message board on 4chan.

569
00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.040
Fit is often kind of a board that will introduce

570
00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:55.640
like young guys to 4chan

571
00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:58.450
because it's guys who are like maybe like 13

572
00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:01.183
or 14 and they're looking to just, you know

573
00:24:01.183 --> 00:24:02.790
kind of like shape up a little bit.

574
00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:04.010
So they'll go to fit.

575
00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:07.160
And they'll sort of like, you know, talk to other young guys

576
00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:10.740
about how to lose weight, how to gain muscle,

577
00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:13.190
how to basically just become men in the world.

578
00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:15.143
And so it seemed like that was probably the moment

579
00:24:15.143 --> 00:24:16.380
that Pepe passed

580
00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.020
from the bodybuilding forum--
In the 4chan community.

581
00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.498
Into the 4chan community.

582
00:24:20.498 --> 00:24:21.331
I remember we we're talking about this,

583
00:24:21.331 --> 00:24:22.570
like COVID tracing, like--

584
00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.900
Well you know it's, to extend the evolutionary analogy.

585
00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:30.100
That's like, you know, the waterborne animal

586
00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:31.973
finally climbing on the land, you know,

587
00:24:31.973 --> 00:24:35.500
it was like a big leap in how this thing ends up evolving.

588
00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:37.890
Well I mean, there isn't emotionally linear line.

589
00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:42.210
I mean, I think you can correlate a lot of this, you know

590
00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:45.850
the in-road is through bodybuilding.com

591
00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:48.220
but it like the metastasis happens

592
00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:51.670
really in like understanding the kinds of people

593
00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:53.080
who are coming to those kinds of forums, right?

594
00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:55.350
Like there's of course a huge number of people

595
00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.940
who are there just to seek out information.

596
00:24:57.940 --> 00:25:02.940
But then there's also like this kind of other

597
00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.350
maybe more pernicious cultural problem,

598
00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:09.180
which is of men who feel like they've been scorned

599
00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.400
specifically by women who are coming onto these boards

600
00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:14.320
to kind of share a struggle and feel

601
00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:18.140
like they have to improve their bodies and kind of like

602
00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:20.360
who kind of see the world and maybe binary ways

603
00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:22.990
and feel like that the only way that they compete

604
00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:26.150
is through like improving themselves physically.

605
00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:28.720
And you start to really see how

606
00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:30.770
like the marrying of the image

607
00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:33.760
of Pepe and the sort of catchphrase of "Feels Good Man"

608
00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:37.120
and then these kind of nascent emotional problems all kind

609
00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:38.900
of coalesced at that moment.
Right.

610
00:25:38.900 --> 00:25:41.460
Yeah and on 4chan there's, I mean,

611
00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:43.430
it's a lot of different message boards and each one

612
00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:45.950
of them kind of have their own little communities attached

613
00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.480
to them, but there's one message board

614
00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:50.240
in particular named r9k.

615
00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:51.800
And this is a place where a lot of guys

616
00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:55.140
would just go basically to kind of like tell a story

617
00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:56.610
about their rejection.

618
00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:58.620
They would go there to talk about how maybe a girl

619
00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.800
didn't sit next to them in the cafeteria

620
00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.390
or about how, you know they'd had a one night stand

621
00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:05.350
and then they felt like rejected afterwards.

622
00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:08.790
And Pepe became the kind of like avatar for that.

623
00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:10.210
And it's an anonymous place.

624
00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.950
People don't use their faces or names.

625
00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:14.150
And Pepe became a stand in for that.

626
00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:16.620
And so he went from the "Feels Good Man", frog

627
00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:20.210
to the feel sad man frog the symbol of rejection.

628
00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:23.370
And so we used Pepe basically

629
00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:26.360
as this like emotional through line to tell the story.

630
00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:29.870
And as Pepe got angrier, the country was getting angrier.

631
00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:34.290
You know, the country was becoming a place where, you know

632
00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:38.172
outside of the mainstream media, there was this aggrievement

633
00:26:38.172 --> 00:26:40.340
that was becoming slightly irrational.

634
00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:43.740
And so we traced Pepe from the feel sad frog

635
00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:46.580
to the feels bad frog to the mad frog to the smug frog

636
00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:47.413
to Donald Trump.

637
00:26:47.413 --> 00:26:49.390
But I know that sounds like a silly narrative device

638
00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:50.690
but it was powerful for us

639
00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:53.050
because ultimately we're trying to make a film

640
00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.030
that tells this emotional journey.

641
00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:56.240
And the thing that's interesting

642
00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:59.030
about Pepe is how people felt attached to the character.

643
00:26:59.030 --> 00:27:01.470
This JPEG really meant something to people.

644
00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.320
And when they started to see the mainstream using it

645
00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:05.800
it felt like a personal affront.

646
00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:06.633
They were like,

647
00:27:06.633 --> 00:27:07.466
that's our thing.
Right.

648
00:27:07.466 --> 00:27:09.040
Like how dare you use our thing?

649
00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:12.140
This has been our safe space to share in amongst ourselves.

650
00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.590
It's not for you.

651
00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:17.480
And, you know, we found that to be interesting.

652
00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:21.270
Like it surprised me going into it, just the connection

653
00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:23.720
that people were feeling

654
00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.100
towards this frog.
Yeah.

655
00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.040
There was--

656
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:27.710
Silly, like as a way to structure

657
00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.280
an otherwise super complicated story.

658
00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:32.520
It actually, as soon as we discovered that parallel

659
00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:35.100
it would became an incredibly powerful narrative device.

660
00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:36.970
And just like, like Arthur just said

661
00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.180
just tracking the descent of Pepe

662
00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:42.840
into a depravity correlated

663
00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:44.480
so neatly with--
Right.

664
00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.300
And then really there's this watershed moment that happens

665
00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:52.090
in 2015 where there's, in the span of two weeks

666
00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:55.720
you have a mass shooting at the Umpqua Community College

667
00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:59.150
in Oregon, which still today is the deadliest mass shooting

668
00:27:59.150 --> 00:28:00.320
in Oregon history.

669
00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:02.990
And supposedly, I mean, this did happen

670
00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:04.030
but we don't know for sure

671
00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:04.863
if the shooter posted it,

672
00:28:04.863 --> 00:28:08.310
but it's in all likelihood.
It seems very likely.

673
00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:11.170
On 4chan, someone posted the day before the shooting

674
00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:12.680
a kind of warning

675
00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.150
don't come to this school, my four chain, friends, whatever.

676
00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.150
And then it had at the bottom

677
00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:18.410
it was an image of Pepe holding a handgun.

678
00:28:18.410 --> 00:28:19.700
And then the next day the shooting happened.

679
00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:21.300
And then that was sort of the first time

680
00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.970
that Pepe really made it into mainstream news.

681
00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:27.550
People referred to him as a Grinch like creature,

682
00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:29.350
but then two weeks later--

683
00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:30.270
And there's a little moment

684
00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:31.740
of like Pepe panic,
Right.

685
00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:34.580
There were a number of colleges that were like, you know

686
00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:36.530
we're like, we're shutting down tomorrow

687
00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:38.610
because we've had this anonymous like threat.

688
00:28:38.610 --> 00:28:40.570
It would be a Pepe image holding a gun

689
00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:42.640
or a Pepe with a ski mask.

690
00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:45.360
Yeah and then two weeks later we had this moment

691
00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:48.440
where Donald Trump retweeted an image of himself

692
00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.360
and it as Pepe.

693
00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:54.960
Yes, he retweeted an image of himself drawn as smug Pepe.

694
00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:57.030
So it was Pepe with like the yellow hair standing

695
00:28:57.030 --> 00:29:01.910
behind the sort of podium as if he was at a press conference

696
00:29:01.910 --> 00:29:04.170
in the Oval Office or in the White House.

697
00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:07.130
And so, and it seemed like the media wasn't kind

698
00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:08.050
of connecting the dots

699
00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:09.500
between those two things,
Right.

700
00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:10.900
You would've thought in an earlier era

701
00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:12.707
that would have like--

702
00:29:12.707 --> 00:29:14.870
But it's like he was dipped in 4chan culture,

703
00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:17.030
you wouldn't be able to decode.

704
00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:17.927
Totally.
Exactly.

705
00:29:17.927 --> 00:29:18.760
And that's exactly what it was.

706
00:29:18.760 --> 00:29:22.540
It was a wink, it was sort of like his, a precursor

707
00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:25.050
to the kind of stand back and standby comment.

708
00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:28.000
Right, it was a very deliberate use of a meme

709
00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:30.760
that was trying to activate a certain group online

710
00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:34.830
who they knew was, you know, starting to gain momentum

711
00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:36.080
as a support base for them, right.

712
00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:41.080
Because you have all these kinds of aggrieved men online

713
00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:43.330
who otherwise it's kind of on its face.

714
00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:45.660
It's kind of ironic because like, there are people

715
00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:47.580
who presumably would get bullied a lot in high school

716
00:29:47.580 --> 00:29:49.940
and like here they are supporting someone

717
00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:51.100
who's kind of like, right.

718
00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:52.580
The biggest bully of all, but for them,

719
00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:53.920
it's like their own bully.

720
00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:58.920
Right and they share a sense of common disdain for women,

721
00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:02.600
people of color, PC culture specifically.

722
00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:04.210
And so they like rally around him.

723
00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.150
And so that moment that Donald Trump tweets himself

724
00:30:07.150 --> 00:30:10.010
as smug Pepe is kind of like, yeah, you're right.

725
00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:11.750
It went completely under the radar, but it's actually

726
00:30:11.750 --> 00:30:15.650
a incredibly significant moment that really the nature

727
00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:16.810
of politics and trolling

728
00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:20.370
just become like completely intertwined.

729
00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:23.200
Because the aggrievement that's felt on 4chan

730
00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:25.140
there's also kind of an entitlement to it.

731
00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:29.590
People feel as though in a previous era,

732
00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:31.690
they would have had a much different life.

733
00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:33.610
They would have like met a girl in high school.

734
00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:35.760
They would have got a job in their hometown

735
00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:39.550
that the sort of like social options

736
00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:41.660
that people have now because of the internet

737
00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:43.960
means that they will be out of work

738
00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:46.680
and unlove for the rest of their lives.

739
00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:49.840
And this is sort of also happening in parallel

740
00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.560
to obviously different parts of the country

741
00:30:51.560 --> 00:30:53.100
feeling as though they're being left out

742
00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:54.740
of the national conversation.

743
00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:56.500
And that part that growing population

744
00:30:56.500 --> 00:31:01.500
of the aggrieved coincides with the maturation

745
00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:04.430
of these platforms that allow these communities

746
00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:08.480
to congregate and unite around these ideas

747
00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:10.830
which is like, you know, that's a very kind

748
00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:14.290
of powerful machine that's humming in the background.

749
00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:17.660
That's basically allowing all of this to percolate

750
00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:19.240
into real life.

751
00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:21.700
Totally I mean, you see, for example

752
00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:23.650
the last Republican national convention

753
00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.340
I think was the first time

754
00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:28.587
that the party didn't put forth an actual platform, right.

755
00:31:28.587 --> 00:31:30.040
It was just, if you watched it

756
00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:35.040
it was just like unbridled, unmitigated anger, and it's kind

757
00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:38.920
of situation that we have now where there is no political

758
00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:41.160
discernible political ideology behind it.

759
00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.590
The only way to coalesce that body

760
00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:45.320
is through iconography and like Pepe,

761
00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:47.730
it really was became the first vehicle

762
00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:48.710
through which that happens.

763
00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:53.210
And then I would argue like Pepe kind of lost its usefulness

764
00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.620
and other things filled in that vacuum,

765
00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.010
whether it's like QAnon

766
00:31:57.010 --> 00:31:58.850
and who knows what it's gonna be in 2021.

767
00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:01.950
But these are, this is kind of the world of politics

768
00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:04.570
and memes that we're having to confront right now.

769
00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:09.570
Well, the inflection point where Pepe tips from 4chan

770
00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:14.070
into broader internet culture is super interesting

771
00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:17.530
you know and kind of backing up from that.

772
00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:19.170
You have this neat culture, right?

773
00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:23.060
That not in education, employment or training, right.

