1529
01:04:12.128 --> 01:04:15.890
Even the people who are lording over the social

1530
01:04:15.890 --> 01:04:18.330
media platforms are befuddled

1531
01:04:18.330 --> 01:04:22.363
or insincere about the path forward.

1532
01:04:23.280 --> 01:04:26.950
Yeah, I mean, I feel like tech has to sort of come away

1533
01:04:26.950 --> 01:04:29.910
from this like startup model or this kind of like boom

1534
01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:32.360
and bust model and think about themselves more

1535
01:04:32.360 --> 01:04:33.500
as like a public utility

1536
01:04:33.500 --> 01:04:35.160
where there has to be different people sitting

1537
01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:35.993
on the boards.

1538
01:04:35.993 --> 01:04:36.970
There has to be at the SIS.

1539
01:04:36.970 --> 01:04:39.660
They have to realize that also, you know

1540
01:04:39.660 --> 01:04:41.440
this is affecting us here in the West,

1541
01:04:41.440 --> 01:04:42.510
but in the global South,

1542
01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:44.940
it's affecting people in an entirely different way.

1543
01:04:44.940 --> 01:04:48.320
You know, in those places where there's not as much

1544
01:04:48.320 --> 01:04:49.960
sort of infrastructure

1545
01:04:49.960 --> 01:04:52.860
this stuff is also raking a different kind of chaos.

1546
01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:57.420
And so, you know, obviously we have to have conversations

1547
01:04:57.420 --> 01:04:59.900
where, you know, we can't have just kind of like

1548
01:04:59.900 --> 01:05:04.520
the Silicon Valley boom and bust control the reality

1549
01:05:04.520 --> 01:05:06.040
of 7 billion people.

1550
01:05:06.040 --> 01:05:07.480
That's just not the way it's gonna work.

1551
01:05:07.480 --> 01:05:09.980
But also the irony, like I've never met Mark Zuckerberg

1552
01:05:09.980 --> 01:05:12.333
but I think he's, you know,

1553
01:05:13.260 --> 01:05:14.930
maybe not the most socialized person.

1554
01:05:14.930 --> 01:05:17.530
So there's just the idea that like our entire worlds

1555
01:05:17.530 --> 01:05:20.450
are being defined by kind of deeply antisocial people

1556
01:05:20.450 --> 01:05:23.680
is really problematic but like the way that we handle

1557
01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:25.620
and confront these issues needs to be taken

1558
01:05:25.620 --> 01:05:26.453
much more seriously.

1559
01:05:26.453 --> 01:05:29.230
Like on Twitter, you know, every tweet at this point

1560
01:05:29.230 --> 01:05:31.070
that Donald Trump puts out has that stupid

1561
01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:32.563
little notification below.

1562
01:05:33.723 --> 01:05:36.180
It's like being standing in front of a tidal wave.

1563
01:05:36.180 --> 01:05:37.740
Right.
It doesn't do it.

1564
01:05:37.740 --> 01:05:39.090
It's ineffective
You just have

1565
01:05:39.090 --> 01:05:39.923
to take them off.

1566
01:05:39.923 --> 01:05:41.710
And like, so many of the things

1567
01:05:41.710 --> 01:05:44.750
you talk about in the movie, it perhaps is even playing

1568
01:05:44.750 --> 01:05:46.420
into the hands of his base.

1569
01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:48.470
Totally.
Like that just

1570
01:05:48.470 --> 01:05:50.110
energizes them right.

1571
01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:50.943
To see that--

1572
01:05:50.943 --> 01:05:52.410
It's more us against them.

1573
01:05:52.410 --> 01:05:56.390
Yeah, you know, you just have to take it more seriously.

1574
01:05:56.390 --> 01:05:57.740
And, you know, I don't know

1575
01:05:57.740 --> 01:06:00.540
to the extent that you have to produce quarterly returns

1576
01:06:01.462 --> 01:06:02.295
for your investors.

1577
01:06:02.295 --> 01:06:05.865
Like I just wonder at what point, I don't know.

1578
01:06:05.865 --> 01:06:08.300
Yeah, well, the anonymous aspect of the whole thing

1579
01:06:08.300 --> 01:06:10.030
is a big part of this, right?

1580
01:06:10.030 --> 01:06:14.170
Like does 4chan exist if you can't post anonymously?

1581
01:06:14.170 --> 01:06:18.040
And there's a argument to be had that every buddy

1582
01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:19.640
who's saying anything on the internet

1583
01:06:19.640 --> 01:06:22.430
should get a blue check and be verified

1584
01:06:22.430 --> 01:06:26.230
so that you can basically legitimize who these people are.

1585
01:06:26.230 --> 01:06:28.880
And yet, you know, I had Bryan Fogel in here talking

1586
01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:31.010
about the dissident the other day and how important it is

1587
01:06:31.010 --> 01:06:36.007
for dissidents to be able to communicate from a safe place.

1588
01:06:36.007 --> 01:06:39.050
And that requires some level of anonymity in a place

1589
01:06:39.050 --> 01:06:40.070
like Saudi Arabia,

1590
01:06:40.070 --> 01:06:43.170
where 80% of the population is on Twitter.

1591
01:06:43.170 --> 01:06:47.100
And the only bastion of free speech is Twitter

1592
01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.730
because it's decentralized and can't be lorded over

1593
01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:52.720
by the control of the kingdom.

1594
01:06:52.720 --> 01:06:54.020
Yeah, I used to think verification

1595
01:06:54.020 --> 01:06:58.930
was a certain path towards, you know, positivity

1596
01:06:58.930 --> 01:07:02.310
but I will say like on all the social media platforms

1597
01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:03.970
probably the worst comments we get

1598
01:07:03.970 --> 01:07:04.803
are on Facebook.
Oh my God.

1599
01:07:04.803 --> 01:07:06.873
And like, I'll just click people's, you know,

1600
01:07:06.873 --> 01:07:10.190
it's a photo of a guy with his family, his two kids

1601
01:07:10.190 --> 01:07:12.010
from Valdosta, Giorgio who's just saying

1602
01:07:12.010 --> 01:07:15.100
like the most appraised worst chat about our film possible.

1603
01:07:15.100 --> 01:07:16.600
It's like maybe

1604
01:07:16.600 --> 01:07:17.550
that one will work.
Have you gotten

1605
01:07:17.550 --> 01:07:20.090
a bunch of negative blow back like that?

1606
01:07:20.090 --> 01:07:22.490
I mean, if you look at the comments on YouTube

1607
01:07:22.490 --> 01:07:24.440
or on Facebook, they're pretty predictable

1608
01:07:24.440 --> 01:07:25.600
Really, yeah.
But I have to say

1609
01:07:25.600 --> 01:07:28.880
that in terms of like our personal engagement with people,

1610
01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:30.040
it's been the opposite.

1611
01:07:30.040 --> 01:07:33.300
Like we've had actually a lot of people reach out and say

1612
01:07:33.300 --> 01:07:34.730
that the movie touched them in some way,

1613
01:07:34.730 --> 01:07:37.090
or they've been spending a lot of time on 4chan,

1614
01:07:37.090 --> 01:07:39.430
maybe when they were a teenager and they're in the process

1615
01:07:39.430 --> 01:07:40.787
of maybe aging out of it.

1616
01:07:40.787 --> 01:07:44.200
And the movie spoke to them in a really positive way.

1617
01:07:44.200 --> 01:07:46.380
You know, I also think like, you know,

1618
01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:48.470
even Pepe in the media has shifted

1619
01:07:48.470 --> 01:07:50.260
since we completed the film.

1620
01:07:50.260 --> 01:07:52.050
Pepe on Twitch is basically like

1621
01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:54.210
the de-facto mascot for Twitch.

1622
01:07:54.210 --> 01:07:55.923
He's back to being a reaction image for people

1623
01:07:55.923 --> 01:07:58.580
that are like excited or happy or angry.

1624
01:07:58.580 --> 01:08:00.360
So if you're on Twitch and you're looking

1625
01:08:00.360 --> 01:08:01.230
at the comments field

1626
01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:03.690
you'll just see like, you know,

1627
01:08:03.690 --> 01:08:05.681
chunks of Pepes floating by--
That have nothing

1628
01:08:05.681 --> 01:08:07.428
to do with like white supremacy

1629
01:08:07.428 --> 01:08:08.261
or anything.
Yeah, at all.

1630
01:08:08.261 --> 01:08:10.040
It's purely just for the walls.

1631
01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:13.330
So, yeah each platform kind of has its own

1632
01:08:13.330 --> 01:08:14.640
Relationship--
Take on things.

1633
01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:17.900
But no we've felt like actually we obviously had some

1634
01:08:17.900 --> 01:08:20.660
understandable fear while we were making the film.

1635
01:08:20.660 --> 01:08:22.670
But the reality is this is a conversation that people

1636
01:08:22.670 --> 01:08:23.503
are kind of ready to have.

1637
01:08:23.503 --> 01:08:25.860
And a lot of that like intense feeling

1638
01:08:25.860 --> 01:08:28.790
that people had about Pepe kind of evaporated

1639
01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:31.100
during 2016 and 2017.

1640
01:08:31.100 --> 01:08:34.300
And this is something that it's time for us

1641
01:08:34.300 --> 01:08:35.654
to all have a conversation about it.

1642
01:08:35.654 --> 01:08:37.450
Right--
'Cause, you know,

1643
01:08:37.450 --> 01:08:40.370
it's something our culture is experiencing and we have to go

1644
01:08:40.370 --> 01:08:41.210
through it together.

1645
01:08:41.210 --> 01:08:42.260
Right.

1646
01:08:42.260 --> 01:08:45.440
So you premiere the movie at Sundance,

1647
01:08:45.440 --> 01:08:46.530
which is super dope.

1648
01:08:46.530 --> 01:08:50.290
Like this is like, you've made one movie prior, right.

1649
01:08:50.290 --> 01:08:52.560
This is the first film that you've

1650
01:08:52.560 --> 01:08:53.490
been involved with it.
Yeah.

1651
01:08:53.490 --> 01:08:56.077
Right, so that's super exciting, right?

1652
01:08:56.077 --> 01:08:57.880
You guys are young guys, you make this movie,

1653
01:08:57.880 --> 01:08:59.820
you get into Sundance, you go,

1654
01:08:59.820 --> 01:09:02.900
you get crazy turnout standing ovation.

1655
01:09:02.900 --> 01:09:04.720
You get, you win this award.

1656
01:09:04.720 --> 01:09:05.950
Yeah, no, it was amazing.

1657
01:09:05.950 --> 01:09:09.610
I mean, also we got into Sundance with like storyboards.

1658
01:09:09.610 --> 01:09:11.700
So a lot of like the animations were just like

1659
01:09:11.700 --> 01:09:13.530
chicken scratch essentially.

1660
01:09:13.530 --> 01:09:17.553
And so we were just like sprinting to get the film done.

1661
01:09:17.553 --> 01:09:20.470
Yeah, but everybody does--
That is true,

1662
01:09:20.470 --> 01:09:23.450
that's like some of my favorite memories from the process

1663
01:09:23.450 --> 01:09:25.110
is those kind of like--
Sleepless.

1664
01:09:25.110 --> 01:09:27.010
Insane nights--
Right.

1665
01:09:27.010 --> 01:09:28.580
Yeah, that was like very emotional.

1666
01:09:28.580 --> 01:09:30.822
Like there was one night where I was like,

1667
01:09:30.822 --> 01:09:32.370
I think it was like five in the morning.

1668
01:09:32.370 --> 01:09:34.867
And we had like, we're trying to mix the film the next day

1669
01:09:34.867 --> 01:09:38.020
and it was just me and our Assistant Editor, Kaitlin,

1670
01:09:38.020 --> 01:09:39.080
in the office.

1671
01:09:39.080 --> 01:09:41.777
And I just told Kaitlin, I was like, "I'm gonna start

1672
01:09:41.777 --> 01:09:42.740
"To cry right now."

1673
01:09:42.740 --> 01:09:44.880
I don't know what else to do, you're just gonna watch me

1674
01:09:44.880 --> 01:09:46.450
and I'm gonna sit here and cry for like an hour

1675
01:09:46.450 --> 01:09:48.780
while we're editing and assembling things,

1676
01:09:48.780 --> 01:09:51.600
because it's such like this emotionally

1677
01:09:51.600 --> 01:09:53.760
kind of intense experience.

1678
01:09:53.760 --> 01:09:56.250
Sundance obviously was like a dream come true.

1679
01:09:56.250 --> 01:09:57.640
But then once you're kind of there,

1680
01:09:57.640 --> 01:09:59.770
you realize there's all these other sort of like--

1681
01:09:59.770 --> 01:10:01.447
Other people with dreams.
It's terrible.

1682
01:10:01.447 --> 01:10:03.723
True and you have many people.

1683
01:10:03.723 --> 01:10:06.560
And you also have to finish the movie and then like throw

1684
01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:08.180
a wedding for the movie at the same time

1685
01:10:08.180 --> 01:10:10.190
because you have to like have a party

1686
01:10:10.190 --> 01:10:11.710
and you have to make sure the party is at right time

1687
01:10:11.710 --> 01:10:14.480
so people will come and it's like, yeah

1688
01:10:14.480 --> 01:10:17.540
it's this really kind of very fun and surreal

1689
01:10:17.540 --> 01:10:20.700
but incredibly stressful thing.

1690
01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:22.330
And then also we're trying to like sell the film.

1691
01:10:22.330 --> 01:10:24.160
So it's kind of we're trying to present ourselves

1692
01:10:24.160 --> 01:10:26.237
as like, "Oh this is a product that we made,

1693
01:10:26.237 --> 01:10:27.990
"Please buy our product"

1694
01:10:27.990 --> 01:10:29.360
Yeah.

1695
01:10:29.360 --> 01:10:30.610
Yeah, it's a heavy experience.

1696
01:10:30.610 --> 01:10:32.120
You go in with the best of intentions

1697
01:10:32.120 --> 01:10:34.440
where like packed house.

1698
01:10:34.440 --> 01:10:36.630
Which theater did you premiere at?

1699
01:10:36.630 --> 01:10:37.730
The pioneer.

1700
01:10:37.730 --> 01:10:39.320
The pioneer, on a Monday.

1701
01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:42.410
Yeah like a Monday morning.

1702
01:10:42.410 --> 01:10:45.020
It doesn't matter what day it is when you're there though.

1703
01:10:45.020 --> 01:10:46.460
Oh, it actually does.

1704
01:10:46.460 --> 01:10:47.859
We found out--
I mean, if you wanna be--

1705
01:10:47.859 --> 01:10:51.000
you wanna be at Egyptian on Friday night or whatever.

1706
01:10:51.000 --> 01:10:54.490
Yeah, I mean the truth is like the big sales all happen

1707
01:10:54.490 --> 01:10:56.720
in the opening two days.

1708
01:10:56.720 --> 01:10:59.160
Right and so like, I think we recognized

1709
01:10:59.160 --> 01:11:02.680
was as soon as we found our Monday slot that like, "Okay."

1710
01:11:02.680 --> 01:11:03.997
I was like you though, I was like,

1711
01:11:03.997 --> 01:11:05.710
"Oh, we're just at Sundance."
Right.

1712
01:11:05.710 --> 01:11:08.650
"Everything is gravy, this is amazing."

1713
01:11:08.650 --> 01:11:09.717
But then it's like, people are like,

1714
01:11:09.717 --> 01:11:11.237
"Well, so-and-so can't come.

1715
01:11:11.237 --> 01:11:13.570
"And they might be interested in film."

1716
01:11:13.570 --> 01:11:15.290
Because it's Monday morning, specifically.

1717
01:11:15.290 --> 01:11:18.016
Oh man, the psychic of anguish, day by day.

1718
01:11:18.016 --> 01:11:22.010
People are just sort of chipping away at your enthusiasm

1719
01:11:22.010 --> 01:11:23.494
as you're kind of going into it.

1720
01:11:23.494 --> 01:11:28.470
And you know, I don't know, I'm a very like kind of private

1721
01:11:28.470 --> 01:11:29.850
self-conscious person,

1722
01:11:29.850 --> 01:11:31.340
like red carpet stuff

1723
01:11:31.340 --> 01:11:33.490
is like uncomfortable for me, for sure.

1724
01:11:33.490 --> 01:11:35.957
And so, there's a part of me that's also like,

1725
01:11:35.957 --> 01:11:37.760
"Well how am I gonna become like,"

1726
01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:39.150
I'm an animator.

1727
01:11:39.150 --> 01:11:41.220
I'm used to like being behind the computer in the middle

1728
01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:42.410
of the night animating.

1729
01:11:42.410 --> 01:11:44.780
I'm not used to sort of like standing up

1730
01:11:44.780 --> 01:11:47.040
and talking constantly and selling a film.

1731
01:11:47.040 --> 01:11:49.250
So that was like a pretty

1732
01:11:49.250 --> 01:11:51.020
heavy experience for me.
Right.

1733
01:11:51.020 --> 01:11:53.660
You know.
I discovered the movie,

1734
01:11:53.660 --> 01:11:55.840
I heard somebody talking about it and it just

1735
01:11:55.840 --> 01:11:57.030
lodged in my memory.

1736
01:11:57.030 --> 01:12:01.170
And so, you know, a couple nights later I searched for it.

1737
01:12:01.170 --> 01:12:02.670
I found it on Amazon.

1738
01:12:02.670 --> 01:12:06.300
I rented it, I watched it, but I did have to look for it.

1739
01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:09.620
Right, it wasn't front and center in my Netflix queue

1740
01:12:09.620 --> 01:12:11.880
screaming at me to watch.

1741
01:12:11.880 --> 01:12:13.880
And you know, as I said at the outset,

1742
01:12:13.880 --> 01:12:16.370
like, I really feel like this,

1743
01:12:16.370 --> 01:12:18.900
you know, this is a movie that everybody should see,

1744
01:12:18.900 --> 01:12:21.510
but I do think that it's suffering

1745
01:12:21.510 --> 01:12:24.770
from a lack of visibility and discoverability.

1746
01:12:24.770 --> 01:12:26.920
So can we talk about like the distribution aspect

1747
01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:27.927
of this whole thing?

1748
01:12:27.927 --> 01:12:29.160
I mean we agree with you--
And that's part

1749
01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:30.510
of why we're here.

1750
01:12:30.510 --> 01:12:33.380
I wanna help you guys out there a little bit.

1751
01:12:33.380 --> 01:12:34.603
Now the conversation

1752
01:12:34.603 --> 01:12:35.717
begins.
Yeah.

1753
01:12:35.717 --> 01:12:38.610
I mean we created our own like distribution company

1754
01:12:38.610 --> 01:12:40.600
basically to--
TO sell out the film.

1755
01:12:40.600 --> 01:12:43.090
We self-distributed the film, so we created a company

1756
01:12:43.090 --> 01:12:44.440
called Ready Fictions,

1757
01:12:44.440 --> 01:12:48.750
In part because you know, we did go into Sundance

1758
01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:50.410
and we'd heard a bunch of different stuff.

1759
01:12:50.410 --> 01:12:52.270
People that were interested in the film,

1760
01:12:52.270 --> 01:12:54.670
but I will say like, this was always a hard movie

1761
01:12:54.670 --> 01:12:56.460
to convince people to get behind.

1762
01:12:56.460 --> 01:12:59.010
And so we were able to make it independently

1763
01:12:59.010 --> 01:12:59.950
which was great

1764
01:12:59.950 --> 01:13:02.080
because we found, I mean, shout out

1765
01:13:02.080 --> 01:13:03.920
to Wavelength Productions,

1766
01:13:03.920 --> 01:13:05.180
they're an independent company

1767
01:13:05.180 --> 01:13:07.620
and they saw some early cuts of the film

1768
01:13:07.620 --> 01:13:10.020
and they always believed in us to make the movie.

1769
01:13:10.020 --> 01:13:12.230
And they have like been very supportive

1770
01:13:12.230 --> 01:13:14.220
but they were supportive in a way that like certainly

1771
01:13:14.220 --> 01:13:16.020
none of the platforms were supportive

1772
01:13:16.020 --> 01:13:18.620
and a lot of the other more established companies

1773
01:13:18.620 --> 01:13:20.420
that we presented the film to.

1774
01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:22.690
And in some cases you could feel like people slowly

1775
01:13:22.690 --> 01:13:25.090
backing out of the room as you were pitching the movie

1776
01:13:25.090 --> 01:13:28.900
because Pepe has this just knee jerk reaction.

1777
01:13:28.900 --> 01:13:31.247
When people see that frog, they're like, "Oh man."

1778
01:13:32.298 --> 01:13:34.460
You know, even one of the programmers at Sundance,

1779
01:13:34.460 --> 01:13:36.917
she was like, "You know, I had all these movies to watch

1780
01:13:36.917 --> 01:13:39.557
"For Sundance and then it was like the Pepe The Frog movie.

